      en: 'The lion lies in a hollow cut into a sandstone cliff, about 10 metres long and 6 metres high. Bertel Thorvaldsen designed it, and the stonemason Lukas Ahorn carved it in 1820 and 1821. It remembers the Swiss Guards killed defending King Louis XVI at the Tuileries Palace in Paris on 10 August 1792. A spear is lodged in the lion\'s side, and a paw rests on a shield with the French lilies. The inscription, "Helvetiorum fidei ac virtuti", means "to the loyalty and bravery of the Swiss". It names 760 dead. Some historians think the real toll was lower. Take a quiet moment.',
